        "original_id": "38781e02-affc-5c66-839d-8ba09396a443",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Lowell George, Ian Hunter, Christopher Cross\n- Bands and musical acts: Little Feat, Mott the Hoople, East Side Beat\n- Songs and works: Ride Like the Wind\n\nFacts:\n- Lowell George was born on April 13, 1945, and he became an American songwriter, multi-instrumentalist, record producer, and the primary guitarist, vocalist, and songwriter for Little Feat.\n- Ian Hunter Patterson was born on 3 June 1939, and Ian Hunter became the lead singer of Mott the Hoople from the band's founding in 1969 to its dissolution in 1974, and again for reunions in 2009 and 2013.\n- Lowell George died on June 29, 1979.\n- Christopher Cross dedicated \"Ride Like the Wind\" on the album's inner sleeve to Lowell George of Little Feat after Lowell George died in 1979.\n- East Side Beat recorded a dance cover of Christopher Cross's \"Ride Like the Wind,\" and the song became a Europe-wide hit that peaked at No. 3 in the UK, their biggest market.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d7e0b1d1-ec2c-5b57-9664-69ffa96e4fb8",
        "text": "Page: Lowell George. Sentence: Lowell Thomas George (April 13, 1945 – June 29, 1979) was an American songwriter, multi-instrumentalist, and record producer, who was the primary guitarist, vocalist, and songwriter for the rock band Little Feat.\nPage: Ian Hunter (singer). Sentence: Ian Hunter Patterson (born 3 June 1939), known as Ian Hunter, is a British singer-songwriter who is best known as the lead singer of the English rock band Mott the Hoople, from its inception in 1969 to its dissolution in 1974, and at the time of its 2009 and 2013 reunions.\nPage: Ride Like the Wind. Sentence: On the album's inner sleeve, Christopher Cross dedicated this song to Lowell George, formerly of the band Little Feat, who had died in 1979.\nPage: East Side Beat. Sentence: This was a dance cover of Christopher Cross's \"Ride Like the Wind\" and became a hit throughout Europe, peaking at No.3 in the UK, which was to be their biggest market.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
